Problem
The 0G compute chat functionality was completely broken due to service discovery failures and provider registration issues. Chat requests would consistently fail with errors like:

No services from contract, using fallback official providers
Provider 0xf07240Efa67755B5311bc75784a061eDB47165Dd failed: execution reverted: ServiceNotExist(address)
All providers failed in race
Root Cause Analysis:

The InferenceServing contract at 0x5299bd255B76305ae08d7F95B270A485c6b95D54 has no registered services
listService() returns an empty array, causing fallback to hardcoded providers
Hardcoded providers fail with "ServiceNotExist(address)" because they're not registered on this contract
The contract ABI was missing the additionalInfo field, causing potential compatibility issues
Static fallback endpoints were using incorrect URLs
Solution
1. Fixed Contract ABI Structure
Updated INFERENCE_SERVING_ABI to match the official 0G SDK structure:

// Added missing additionalInfo field to ServiceStruct
{
  "components": [
    { "internalType": "address", "name": "provider", "type": "address" },
    // ... other fields
    { "internalType": "string", "name": "additionalInfo", "type": "string" } // Added
  ]
}
2. Enhanced Service Discovery with Robust Fallback Chain
Completely rewrote the service discovery logic in chat-service.ts:

// 1. Try contract getAllServices()
const services = await broker.inference.listService()

// 2. If empty, try individual getServiceMetadata() for known providers  
for (const providerAddr of OFFICIAL_PROVIDER_ADDRESSES) {
  const metadata = await broker.inference.getServiceMetadata(providerAddr)
  // Use contract-provided endpoint and model
}

// 3. If providers not registered, fall back to multiple API endpoints
const possibleEndpoints = [
  'https://api.0g.ai',
  'https://inference.0g.ai', 
  'https://compute.0g.ai',
  'https://serving.0g.ai'
]
3. Multiple Endpoint Support
When providers aren't registered on the contract, the service now tries multiple possible 0G API endpoints instead of failing completely. This provides resilience against endpoint changes or network issues.

4. Enhanced Error Handling and Rate Limiting
Added strategic delays to prevent RPC throttling
Implemented provider acknowledgment caching (10-minute TTL)
Enhanced logging for better debugging
Graceful degradation instead of complete failure
